  year = {2024},
  doi = {10.25439/rmt.27597720},
  abstract = {My practice-based research project develops and demonstrates artistic strategies for the composition of non-naturalistic visual depth in stereoscopic virtual reality (VR). I accomplish this through a series of creative experiments and VR experiences. Each work is rendered in real time and experienced using head-mounted display technology and six degrees of freedom head tracking. Through these works I challenge the viewers binocular depth perception by altering the spatial depiction of the three-dimensional virtual environment. Stereoscopic imagery has traditionally been bound to the rectangular frame of the cinematic screen format. This framing is known as the stereo window and has historically been a limiting compositional problem in 3D cinema. When the 3D image intersects with the edge of the frame, the illusion of depth collapses. More recently, head-mounted displays place the proximity of the viewers eyes closer to the display, effectively eliminating the rectangular framing of the screen from view, along with all visual reference to the outside world. The positional head tracking allows the user to interactively control their viewpoint in the 360-degree stereoscopic phenomenon that surrounds them. Subjectively, VR offers the illusion that the user can enter and inhabit a stereoscopic virtual environment. It usually employs a strategy of normative depth emulation that seeks to maximise immersion and a sense of presence. In contrast, I approach the composition of stereoscopic virtual environments as a layered meta-composition of multiple realities coexisting simultaneously. Working at the intersection of stereoscopic media, virtual reality, and non-naturalistic spatial representation in the visual arts, my research is motivated by the following question: What does stereoscopic VR offer in terms of unique spatial depth strategies and how can it be used to create experiences of simultaneity and multiplicity? I address this question using a reflective practitioner methodology involving rapid prototyping of ideas, critical self-reflection, and iterative development. Specifically, I re-examine traditional cinematic practices of stereoscopic image construction to expand the possibilities of depth composition in VR. Through my practice I develop an artistic strategy I call the depth object. A depth object transforms the stereo window from a default rectangle into a 3D object that appears within a 360-degree virtual environment. This introduces the potential for more complex silhouettes forframing. The depth objects rendered in the scene both occlude and reveal through the renderingof multiple layers simultaneously. These overlapping 3D objects provide layers of viewing portalsto other virtual environments. Depth objects are designed to confound our normative experienceof near and far by weaving together new spatial relationships. The depth object approach to 3D composition subverts the natural order of vision and presents the audience with a surprisi},
  collection = {Theses},
  note = {Found in DataCite; selected by similarity to the archives; NIME-topic probability 0.86}
}

@phdthesis{thesis:ebenreuter2024transferenceofda,
  author = {Ebenreuter, Natalie},
  title = {Transference of dance knowledge through interface design},
  school = {Swinburne},
  year = {2024},
  doi = {10.25916/sut.26261831.v1},
  abstract = {This thesis is concerned with the careful arrangement and organization of technical content. I argue that subject matter in the context of observation or ideation acts in concert with the abilities of the designer and available materials to shape the form of a product with its functional purpose and relevance. To assist the inquiry into the treatment of form and matter, a poetic strategy has been adopted which includes elements of dialectic, rhetoric, and grammar to better understand the requirements of design and to formulate a solution. Within this framework, a prototype application, “LabanAssist,” has been designed to provide dancers, choreographers, artistic directors, choreologists, students, and educators with a tool designed to enhance dance literacy through greater provision and accessibility of the dance notation system “Labanotation.” The ephemeral nature of dance and the absence of a widely acknowledged system to provide an objective record of dance movement have contributed to the scarce historical references to dance material (Calvert, Coyle, and Maranan, 2002). An increasing awareness of the drivers surrounding the preservation of movement highlights the necessity to effectively preserve dance works that risk being contaminated or lost (Wang, 2004). The integration of technology into the arts motivated the development of complex computer applications that supply artists with a greater means of creative expression (Assey, 2005). Movement can be effectively documented by the use of dance notation. Languages such as Labanotation provide a precise system of recording movement; analogous to the techniques musicians employ to notate music (Calvert et al., 2002). Current literature emphasises that existing dance notation applications are not equipped to detect or prevent errors made during the composition of Labanotation scores. These dance notation applications require an expert knowledge of Labanotation to operate effectively (T. Calvert, I. Fox, R. Ryman, and L. Wilke, 2005), fuelling the risk of further contamination as dance knowledge is transferred to a digital environment. This research proceeds on the basis that the integration of an operational structure for the documentation of movement within the prototype application LabanAssist can ensure that the correct syntax of dance notation is established. Coupled with the visual interpretation of notated movement in an immediate environment, LabanAssist functions as a diagnostic tool in which novice users of Labanotation may evaluate their notation and more easily interpret errors in their notation. LabanAssist has been tested in the dance community to assess levels of user response, understanding, accessibility, and capability.},
  collection = {Theses},
  note = {Found in DataCite; selected by similarity to the archives; NIME-topic probability 0.95}
}

@phdthesis{thesis:mcmillan2025accessibilityinm,
  author = {McMillan, Andrew Strachan},
  title = {Accessibility in Music-Making},
  school = {University of Auckland},
  year = {2025},
  url = {https://hdl.handle.net/2292/76588},
  abstract = {This doctoral thesis investigates the intersection of accessibility and creativity in music-making, focusing on the design of bespoke instruments for disabled musicians. The research is based within the University of Auckland’s music department. It aims to answer several key questions: Research question one (RQ1): What specific needs must be considered when creating a bespoke instrument for myself as a disabled musician? Question two (RQ2): How do design affordances and creative requirements interact? Question three (RQ3): How can current internet and network technologies help disabled musicians form and sustain creative collaborations and connections? Using a mixed-methods approach, the study combines autoethnographic reflection with a review of relevant literature in accessibility, design, and music technology. The research process involved prototyping and iterative testing of a custom instrument tailored to my unique physical and artistic needs. This process underscored the importance of user-centred design, adaptability, and the integration of assistive technologies. The thesis examines how design affordances - ergonomic features, interface customisation, and responsiveness - must be balanced against creative requirements to enhance expressive potential, versatility, and aesthetic factors. These findings highlight that effective bespoke instruments are developed through a dialogue between the physical realities of disability and the artistic ambitions of the musician. In exploring the role of the internet and network technologies, the PhD thesis identifies a variety of tools and platforms that support remote collaboration and community building amongst disabled musicians. The technologies discussed assist in reducing barriers to participation and foster inclusive creative networks. The research concludes that well-informed instrument design, guided by technological possibilities and creative goals, can enable disabled musicians to fully engage in collaborative music-making. This work provides practical recommendations for instrument designers, artists, educators, and technologists aiming to improve accessibility and innovation in music-making while confronting disability-related barriers.},
  collection = {Theses},
  note = {Found in OpenAlex; selected by similarity to the archives; NIME-topic probability 0.77}
}

