  abstract = {"Since the mid-20th century, performers and composers have increasingly integrated electronics into their creative practices—a trend that continues to flourish in the 21st century. Advances in music technology, sound recording, and media arts have continually shaped contemporary music, creating a growing need for performers and composers to engage with these tools. Many artists are now exploring the augmentation of traditional instruments as a way to expand performance practice beyond its conventional roots. A central challenge in this domain is the rapid pace at which new technologies enter artistic practice. While tools such as microphones have become standard, emerging technologies— including gestural controllers and video processing—are becoming increasingly significant to contemporary performance practice. These rapid developments make it difficult to establish stable frameworks without engaging in extensive experimentation. In response, I have led a series of artistic commissions, improvisations, and research projects employing a wide array of tools. This research-creation project both demonstrates current practices in instrument augmentation and generates practical applications of these technologies. The resulting repertoire serves as a corpus for comparative analysis, supported by visualization tools such as multi-axis dimension space charts created to address the inherent ambiguity of this practice. By investigating and analyzing a broad spectrum of tools and processes, this research proposes strategies for navigating the complexity of instrument augmentation and offers a comprehensive foundation for ongoing artistic and pedagogical exploration as the field continues to evolve. III Abrégé Depuis le milieu du XXe siècle, les interprètes et les compositeurs intègrent de plus en plus des outils technologiques dans leurs pratiques créatives, une tendance qui ne cesse de s’accentuer au XXIe siècle. Les avancées en technologie musicale, en enregistr