  year = {2025},
  doi = {10.71781/4400},
  abstract = {This thesis presents the artistic and technical approaches used to create a series of interactive audiovisual works based on machine learning. These creations explore how body movement allows for intuitive interaction with latent sound spaces. These spaces correspond to multidimensional representations automatically generated from sound banks. The presented works rely on gestural control and concatenative synthesis to sensitively explore complex sonic relationships, such as timbral proximity or morphological continuity. The creative process is illustrated by three studies, Hypnagogia, Synapses, and Qualia, as-well as a large-scale work, Spectra, which explores gesture and movement within latent sonic spaces. These works establish different forms of relationship between sound and image, articulated by the moving body and real-time interaction. Inspired by systems theory and artificial intelligence, this research-creation project uses various algorithms to analyze, organize, and explore sound banks using gestural interfaces. In this experimental artistic framework, the computer is considered a co-creation partner: agency is distributed between humans and machines, and the form of the works emerges directly from this interaction. This systemic approach opens new perspectives for interactive audiovisual performances through the integration of machine learning algorithms.},
